    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d237">

                <pb facs="0223" n="173" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg173"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.939/32</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morris</hi>)<anchor n="4" xml:id="d237-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-02-04T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 4,
                                1918, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>On January 28 the American Minister at Peking telegraphed that the Chinese
                    Government had prohibited all exports from Manchuria into Siberia. He thought it
                    probable, however, that exception would be made in favor of merchandise in
                    transit to Vladivostok for export.</p>
                <p>On February 2 the Department instructed the American Minister at Peking that the
                    American Government could not assent to the prohibition of the export to Siberia
                    of merchandise owned by Americans. He was instructed so to inform the Foreign
                    Office and say that in the opinion of this Government any embargo, particularly
                    at this <pb facs="0224" n="174" xml:id="pg174"/> time, on exports to Siberia was
                    inadvisable and his attention was called to the attitude of this Government
                    towards the Bolsheviki as set forth in the President’s recent address to
                    Congress and in the Department’s telegram of January 21, 7 p.m., which was a
                    copy of one sent to you January 20, 6 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d237-f2"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Polk</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d237-f1" n="4" type="source" xml:id="d237-f1-content">
                <p>The same, <hi rendition="#italic">mutatis mutandis</hi>, on the same date, to the
                    Ambassadors in France (No. 3141) and Great Britain (No. 6441), and to the Consul
                    at Vladivostok.</p>
            </note>
